WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.886
Secure containers start before deployment.

2
00:00:02.886 --> 00:00:12.833
Base image selection brings operating system packages, libraries, defaults, user accounts, and potential vulnerabilities into the application image.

3
00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:18.832
A trusted, minimal, well-maintained base image reduces what the runtime container carries.

4
00:00:18.832 --> 00:00:28.704
An image full of unused package managers, shells, debugging tools, stale libraries, or build artifacts increases both exposure and maintenance burden.

5
00:00:28.704 --> 00:00:35.158
Image provenance answers where an image came from, how it was built, and whether it should be trusted.

6
00:00:35.158 --> 00:00:45.258
Teams should prefer known sources, controlled registries, reviewable build pipelines, immutable image references where practical, and clear ownership.

7
00:00:45.258 --> 00:00:48.219
Tags are convenient labels, but they can move.

8
00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:56.724
Immutability concepts, digests, image signing, and verification help teams reason about exactly what artifact is being deployed.

9
00:00:56.724 --> 00:01:01.584
Dependency management and vulnerability scanning are ongoing practices.

10
00:01:01.584 --> 00:01:09.253
Scanning can identify known vulnerable packages in images and dependencies, but it is not a complete security program.

11
00:01:09.253 --> 00:01:14.265
Results need triage, rebuild workflows, exception handling, and ownership.

12
00:01:14.265 --> 00:01:26.870
Software bills of materials, at a high level, help teams understand what components are present so vulnerability response and compliance reviews are grounded in inventory rather than guesswork.

13
00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:32.717
Runtime images should avoid unnecessary secrets, tools, shells, and build-only content.

14
00:01:32.717 --> 00:01:40.539
Multi-stage builds, careful copy steps, and clean dependency practices can produce smaller and more focused artifacts.

15
00:01:40.539 --> 00:01:51.625
Registry access control is also part of supply chain hygiene: unauthorized pulls can expose sensitive artifacts, and unauthorized pushes can introduce untrusted images.

16
00:01:51.625 --> 00:01:57.396
The image should be treated as production software, not as a disposable packaging detail.
